---
import HelpLayout from '../../layouts/HelpLayout.astro';

/**
 * SEO / positioning page (research Part 12 P7, spec section 4 of badger-fit
 * docs/superpowers/specs/2026-07-18-m-web-4-orphaned-pages.md). Part 7 found
 * that the privacy demand people actually type is "no subscription" and "no
 * account", which is why this page leads with money rather than with privacy.
 *
 * HONEST ROUNDUP, binding: the competitor table describes shapes (subscription,
 * capped free tier) and never prices, because prices go stale and a wrong number
 * about someone else's product is worse than no number. Each entry says what is
 * good about the app as well as what it costs. The caveat paragraph and the
 * checked-on date under the table are not decoration: they are what makes the
 * table honest six months from now. Re-check the date when you touch a row.
 *
 * Source for the competitor rows: badger-fit
 * docs/competitive_research_2026-07.md Parts 5 and 9 (store listings and public
 * review corpora, gathered 2026-07-13).
 *
 * Badger's own claims were checked against the app source and pubspec on
 * 2026-07-25: no billing, ads, or analytics dependency exists in the project.
 * The "catch" section is deliberately as long as the good news.
 */

const description =
  'Badger is a workout tracker with no subscription, no account, and no caps on routines or history. What free actually means here, where the app falls short, and how the other trackers charge.';

const others = [
  {
    name: 'FitNotes',
    body: 'Genuinely free, no subscription, no ads, Android only. Development runs at roughly a release a year, so it is a stable simple log rather than a growing app. If you are on Android and it already does what you want, it does what you want.',
  },
  {
    name: 'Hevy',
    body: 'A well-made app with a generous free tier that is nonetheless capped: a limited number of saved routines, limits on custom exercises, and older measurement history held behind Hevy Pro, which is a subscription. Plenty of people happily pay for it.',
  },
  {
    name: 'Strong',
    body: 'The long-established one. The free tier is limited to a small number of routine templates, and the rest sits behind Strong Pro. It also requires an account, which together with its watch sync is what a lot of its unhappy reviews are about.',
  },
  {
    name: 'StrongLifts 5x5',
    body: 'Now subscription-first: its store listing states the app requires a subscription to use. A lot of its recent reviews are from long-time users who bought a one-time or lifetime unlock years ago and found it retired. Those are worth reading whichever app you end up picking.',
  },
];
---

<HelpLayout title="Workout tracker with no subscription" description={description}>
  <h1>A workout tracker with no subscription</h1>

  <p>
    "No subscription" turned into something you have to check, because a tracker that was free when
    you picked it three years ago may not be free now, and the history you put into it may not be
    yours to read anymore. Fair question to ask up front. This page answers it for Badger, including
    the parts that are not flattering, and says how the trackers people usually weigh up against it
    charge.
  </p>

  <h2 id="what-badger-costs">What Badger costs</h2>

  <p>
    Nothing. It is free on Google Play, with an iOS beta open in TestFlight. There is no paid tier to
    graduate to, and free here means all of the following, not just the first one.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>No account needed.</strong> No sign-up, no login, no email address. You install it and
      start logging.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>No subscription and no in-app purchase.</strong> There is no Pro version, no trial
      running down, and nothing in the app that asks you to upgrade.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>No ads and no data collection.</strong> The app ships with no analytics and nothing that
      phones home. This website runs no trackers and sets no cookies either, which is also why it
      cannot tell how many people read this page.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>No caps.</strong> Unlimited routines, unlimited history, unlimited custom exercises.
      Your tenth routine and your fourth year of training are not a paid feature.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Export is not behind a paywall.</strong> Every set exports to CSV, the whole database
      backs up to a single file you keep wherever you like, and routines and gym setups share as
      plain files. Getting your numbers out is not something you pay to unlock.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Those five are written up properly, in the form you can hold us to, on
    <a href="/promise">the Badger promise</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="why">Why there is nothing to charge for</h2>

  <p>
    Not principle so much as arithmetic. Badger has no servers. No account means no user database, no
    sync engine, no storage bill, and no monthly cost that has to be recovered from you. Your training
    sits in a local database on your phone, which is why the app is free and also why it does not sync
    between your devices. The same decision produces both, and it would be dishonest to sell you the
    first without mentioning the second.
  </p>

  <p>
    Beyond that: Badger is one person's project, with no investors, no growth target, and nothing to
    pay back. That is a real answer to "what is the catch", and it is also the reason the promise page
    can commit to not taking a feature away and selling it back to you later.
  </p>

  <h2 id="the-catch">The catch</h2>

  <p>
    Everything has one. Here is Badger's, in full, so you can decide against it now rather than after
    you have imported four years of history.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>No sync between devices.</strong> One phone at a time. There is an optional backup to a
      private folder in your own Google Drive, and a plain database file you can move yourself, but
      two devices do not stay in step. See <a href="/help/phone-migration">moving to a new phone</a>.
    </li>
    <li><strong>No web app and no desktop version.</strong> The phone is the whole product.</li>
    <li><strong>No Apple Watch or Wear OS app.</strong> You cannot log a set from your wrist.</li>
    <li>
      <strong>iOS is a TestFlight beta today</strong>, not an App Store release. Android is the
      mature, most-tested platform.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>No social feed</strong>, no followers, no leaderboards, no streak badges nagging you
      back in. Some people want those.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>No AI coach inside the app.</strong> What Badger does instead is hand you your own data
      in formats you can give to whichever assistant you already pay for, with prompts to start from:
      <a href="/guides/ai">use your own AI</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Support is one person and an email address.</strong> Not a team, not a 24-hour SLA.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="the-others">How the other trackers charge</h2>

  <p>
    An honest roundup, not a takedown. These are good apps that made different decisions about money,
    and for a lot of people the right pick is one of them.
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Tracker</th>
          <th>What the free version gets you</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          others.map((o) => (
            <tr>
              <td>
                <strong>{o.name}</strong>
              </td>
              <td>{o.body}</td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p class="fineprint">
    Checked July 2026. Free tiers and prices change often, sometimes sharply, so treat this as a
    starting point and read the app's own store listing before you decide anything. No prices are
    quoted here on purpose: a stale number about somebody else's product is worse than none.
  </p>

  <p>
    The difference worth understanding is not the monthly figure. It is what happens to your training
    history the day you stop paying. Where the history lives on a company's servers, that is their
    decision to make. With Badger there is nothing to decide, because it was never anywhere but your
    phone, and there is no plan to take something you already use and put a price on it.
  </p>

  <h2 id="leaving">If you are coming from one of them</h2>

  <p>
    Bring the history with you. Badger imports from FitNotes, Strong, Hevy, and StrongLifts, straight
    from the export file each of them produces, and the import runs on your phone without sending
    anything anywhere. Your PRs and graphs are recalculated from what arrives, so they start out full.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li><a href="/guides/switching">Switching from another app</a> walks through the first five minutes.</li>
    <li>
      <a href="/help/getting-started#importing">Importing from another app</a> has the per-app export
      instructions.
    </li>
    <li>
      <a href="/guides/stronglifts-5x5-tracker">StrongLifts 5x5 in Badger</a> covers that migration in
      particular, including what the CSV does not carry.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="suspicious">If free makes you suspicious</h2>

  <p>
    It should. A free app usually means the product is you, and asking where the money comes from is
    the right instinct. In this case there is no advertising business, no analytics being collected,
    no data to sell because none of it is held anywhere to sell, and no venture money waiting for a
    return. If any of that ever changes, <a href="/promise">the promise</a> is the thing to hold it
    against. It is written down for exactly that reason.
  </p>
</HelpLayout>

<style>
  /* Same wrapper the format page uses: a two-column table fits a phone, but the
     wrapper means a long unbreakable string scrolls inside its own box instead of
     widening the page. Cheap insurance against sideways scroll at 360px. */
  .table-wrap {
    max-width: 100%;
    overflow-x: auto;
  }

  /* The first column is a name, so it never needs to wrap mid-word and looks
     wrong when it does. The second column takes the rest. */
  .table-wrap td:first-child {
    white-space: nowrap;
    padding-right: 1rem;
  }

  /* Caveats and date-stamps read as fine print, deliberately quieter than the
     table they qualify. --text-muted is AA-checked against the ground. */
  .fineprint {
    font-size: 13px;
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }
</style>
